WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.529
The opening trap of Saw 4 takes place in a
mausoleum, and some of the tomb markers are

00:00:03.529 --> 00:00:06.451
named after the crew members who helped make
the movie, like the art department’s painter

00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:10.560
Napoleon Forbes, the carpenters Hubert Peddle
and Brian Lumley, and a miscellaneous crew

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.650
member named Cris Becker.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:15.490
It makes sense to see carpenters and painters
immortalize themselves on the physical set,

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.830
while someone like the graphic designer Sean
Scoffield got his own name in there on this

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.230
fingerprint analysis report.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:23.960
The head of the forensics department is listed
as David Armstrong, who happens to be Saw

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.480
4’s Director of Photography, which is also
considered to be the head of the camera department

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.939
and electric department on a movie.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:32.680
There is also some information on this report
that answers a previously unsolved mystery

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.780
about Saw 2, so stick around if you want to
hear about it.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.140
[INTRO]

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:41.469
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:00:41.469 --> 00:00:44.260
Saw 4 represents a transition for the Saw
Franchise.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.510
After the departure of the original writer,
this would be the last Saw movie director

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.990
Darren Lynn Bousman would direct for the next
13 years.

00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:54.039
But the producers obviously wanted to keep
the series going, and as a result, Saw started

00:00:54.039 --> 00:00:55.850
to become more... episodic.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.239
The movie would contain setups that might
not pay off for another two or three movies

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:01.079
and some of them are pretty well kept secrets.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:06.429
I’m gonna uncover and talk about even more
Things You Missed, so let's put those words.

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:08.139
On your screen.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:08.820
Right now.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.420
The film opens with John Kramer’s autopsy,
if only this guy knew how valuable that mask

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.820
would become in 2020, and the scene sets
up for the big twist of this movie, the fact

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.729
that the autopsy is actually the last scene
chronologically, and Saw 4 takes place at

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:25.229
the same time as Saw 3.

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:28.249
The tape in John’s stomach is played for
Detective Hoffman, and you can fully expect

00:01:28.249 --> 00:01:31.320
a YouTube video from me in about 70 years
with a similar message.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.920
“You think it’s over just because I’m
dead?

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.770
I promise that my work will continue.”

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:43.429
Despite this twist being a surprise to most,
we are constantly shown that time is an important

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:44.709
factor in Saw 4.

00:01:44.709 --> 00:01:48.289
From the ancient clock that John emphasizes
to Jill, to the watch he angrily throws away

00:01:48.289 --> 00:01:52.419
after losing their unborn son, to the countdown
that looms over this movie’s main game,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.079
to the theme of that game being about time
and patience…

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.340
“Time was on your side, but your obsession
wouldn’t let you wait.”

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:04.119
...to many of the lessons that John teaches
to his victims also being about time.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:07.240
“Give me time to explain, please!”

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.740
“I can’t give you time.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:09.680
No one can.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:10.939
Time is an illusion.”

00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:14.580
But outside of the concept of time, there
are also a lot of clues that the events of

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.610
Saw 3 had not yet taken place.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:19.769
Like the fact that outside of that opening
flash-forward scene, there’s no mention

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:23.280
of John and Amanda’s death, and the most
recent headlines we see are all about the

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.540
events of Saw 2, like this one that says “Detective
and Serial Killer Still Missing,” a reference

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:31.319
to Matthews and John literally dipping out
so that John can take the Detective to the Nerve

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:32.319
Gas House.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:35.859
Also, the photos on the left side are all
photos from John’s workshop at Wilson Steel

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:36.859
Plant.

00:02:36.859 --> 00:02:40.299
If this was taking place after John Kramer’s
autopsy, they would also have photos from

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:41.439
the Operation Room.

00:02:41.439 --> 00:02:45.310
This magazine cover has an article about Detective
Matthews in relation to Jigsaw, but nothing

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:49.700
about Dr. Denlon, who, by the way is mentioned
off hand in the movie but we don’t know

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:50.700
it’s her at the time.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.340
“Boss, it looks like another doctor went
missing from the hospital.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.660
You think maybe it’s uhh…?”

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.160
“Thanks.”

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.860
When I heard this line, I thought he was saying
another doctor as in, another doctor in addition

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:02.819
to Dr. Denlon, but it was really another doctor
in addition to Dr. Gordon.

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:05.319
Shortly after, Hoffman is seen holding a children’s
toy.

00:03:05.319 --> 00:03:08.810
When asked about it by Agent Perez, he simply
says, “it’s a short story, believe me,”

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:12.569
which is not a lie, but if we look closely
at it, it’s actually one of the toys that

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:15.400
belongs to Corbett Denlon, the kidnapped daughter
from Saw 3.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.180
And the Saw 3 connections really don’t let
up because Saw 4 straight up shows us Hoffman

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.810
placing a note for Amanda in the middle drawer
of John’s desk, which is a connection to

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:24.700
this line from the previous movie.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.220
"In my desk.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:29.940
The center drawer.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.760
An envelope.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.040
Your name on it."

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.469
It seems that Darren Lynn Bousman, who I apparently
can’t talk at all about without saying his

00:03:38.469 --> 00:03:42.430
full first, middle and last name, was so confident
that we wouldn’t notice these details that

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.150
he straight up put the date that this all
takes place on: April 28th 2006.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.870
While we don’t see the exact date in Saw
3, we know it can’t be that long after Saw

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:53.432
2, where “John Kramer was damn near on his
deathbed the last time we saw him.”

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:56.329
We also know that Saw 4 takes place 6 months
after Saw 2.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:59.409
“Eric’s been missing for 6 months, Kerry’s
been gone for 4 days.”

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:02.950
So the timeline checks out; it makes sense
that 3 and 4 are taking place on the same

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:03.950
day.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:07.639
Also, the date helps timeline obsessed people
such as myself confirm when Saw 2 actually

00:04:07.639 --> 00:04:11.549
takes place, because Saw 4 is stated to be
six months after the disappearance of Eric

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:12.549
Matthews.

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:15.659
Previously, we could only estimate that Saw
2 took place in October 2005, based on the

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:18.799
decomposition of Adam and Zep’s bodies in
the bathroom; they look like they’ve been

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:20.109
rotting for about a year.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.500
But now we have something more concrete.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.450
And it fits right in with this newspaper headline
stating that police have released the identity

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.780
of Jigsaw, and it’s published on November
30th.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.890
It makes sense that they would keep it under
wraps after first discovering that John Kramer

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:34.750
was Jigsaw, but after losing him and being
unable to find him for the next month, they

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:36.479
would release his identity to the public.

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:40.470
Also, the writer of this other article is
Jane Kim, the assistant of Darren Lynn Bousman

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.470
on this movie.

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:45.060
There’s another article later on written
by Jason Lunn, the leadman of the Art Department.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.860
Of course, the big clue where I think most
of us started to realize that Saw 4 took place

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:52.789
alongside the third installment was at the
end, when Agent Strahm shows up to Gideon

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:56.430
Meat Packing Plant and finds the photo of
Jeff’s family seen in the previous film.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:57.550
Then there’s more of…

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.720
Jeff abusing the lights for some reason.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:03.620
He really does not like lights, does he?

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.340
So there is a lot of evidence to look back
on that proves Saw 4’s timeline placement,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.430
and while Saw 4 isn’t among my favorite
Saw movies, I think there’s something that

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:13.949
I like about giving an iconic entry in a franchise
multiple direct sequels.

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:17.949
It reminds me of the Legend of Zelda franchise,
where they came out with this legendary game,

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:21.710
Ocarina of Time, and gave one sequel in the
timeline branch where Link is a child, and

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:24.330
one sequel in the timeline branch he abandoned
as an adult.

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:27.830
But the interesting thing about Saw 4 is it
not only moves us forward in the timeline,

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:32.009
but also flashes us back to John Kramer’s
past, where we can discover even more Things

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:32.960
You Missed.

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.280
When Officer Rigg is watching back the Jill
Tuck interrogation tapes, he does so on reference

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.099
monitor B at the police station.

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:53.750
We can also see there is another monitor on
this cart, but the label says 9 inch HD Monitor.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.941
Sometimes in the movie industry, you can get
away with using equipment as props, and this

00:05:56.941 --> 00:05:58.389
is probably one of those cases.

00:05:58.389 --> 00:06:02.830
In fact, it happens a lot in Saw 4, like later
on at Rigg’s house and maybe here at the

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:06.819
Ice Block Trap, though it’s possible these
Lite Panels were never supposed to be on-screen.

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:10.630
When you make 7 movies in 7 years, that’s
the kind of mistake that can happen.

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.849
When we first see the newspaper article about
John buying the Gideon Meat Packing Plant,

00:06:13.849 --> 00:06:17.330
there’s actually some nice lore, as opposed
to the first Saw where the contents of the

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:18.740
articles were just random stories.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.270
Apparently, the building had sat abandoned
for 100 years before John purchased it as

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:26.110
part of his Urban Renewal Group project, which
could explain why nobody ever discovered what

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.200
he was doing there, and why the majority of
people don't really even know where it was.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.000
“Where is it?

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:31.500
Where is it?

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:32.080
WHERE IS IT?

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:33.260
Where... is it...?

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:33.960
WHERE IS IT?!?"

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.800
The main game that we follow during Saw 4
is the Trial of Daniel Rigg, which contained

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:39.840
a lot of parallels to the original Saw.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.620
For example, Rigg’s game begins when he
wakes up in a bathtub, just like Adam Stanheight

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:45.139
when he began his Jigsaw journey.

00:06:45.139 --> 00:06:49.030
There would be another Adam parallel later,
when Rigg comes upon a room filled with photos

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:53.430
of the crimes that took place at a slimy motel,
whose red light illumination is very reminiscent

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.330
of the darkroom in Adam’s apartment, which
is similarly lined with incriminating candid

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:58.330
photos.

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:02.069
On a side note, Jigsaw’s punishment for
Ivan is to strap him down to this bed?

00:07:02.069 --> 00:07:06.289
I mean… does he not realize there are people
who’ll pay good money for that kind of experience.

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:10.050
There are a few more callbacks to the original,
like the question marks seen throughout Rigg’s

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:13.020
adventure, which reference the question mark
painted on Donnie Greco’s stomach for the

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.129
reverse bear trap.

00:07:14.129 --> 00:07:17.539
I’ve mentioned throughout this series that
the franchise has many instances of a character

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:21.770
playing dead in order to make a surprise attack,
and that comes back again in this one as Detective

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:25.020
Matthews feigns death to get close to his
supposed captor, Art Blank.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.669
And then at the Chinese New Year celebration,
which is of course Year of the Pig, we see

00:07:28.669 --> 00:07:32.169
the origin of the pig masks worn by John’s
followers in all four Saw movies.

00:07:32.169 --> 00:07:35.490
He starts off by using a more cartoony pig
mask, but in the background there are more

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:39.669
realistic ones like he would eventually come
to use, and what I found very interesting

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:43.810
was another mask with spiral patterns on the
cheeks like that of Billy the Puppet.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.610
This must just be a coincidence though, because
he already had created Billy as a toy for

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.930
his unborn child prior to these events.

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:54.270
Unless he had maybe seen this mask elsewhere
before and maybe was inspired by it.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.569
Another likely coincidental connection found
in the Chinese New Year Parade scene is these

00:07:57.569 --> 00:08:00.889
little figurines, which can also be seen beside
John’s deathbed in Saw 3.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:01.889
But back to Rigg.

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:05.000
He comes out of his bathroom to find his house
has been turned into a trap, and the phone

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:06.000
has been taken.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.979
I wonder if Jigsaw re-used the audio of the
voicemail greeting from Saw 2?

00:08:08.979 --> 00:08:12.410
“You've reached Daniel’s phone, he's not
in right now but if you--.”

00:08:12.410 --> 00:08:15.409
I should start selling those voicemail greetings
on Fiverr or something.

00:08:15.409 --> 00:08:18.909
He comes up to his first test subject, Brenda,
who is trapped in the scalp chair, and he

00:08:18.909 --> 00:08:21.289
discovers the three digit combination to release
her.

00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:22.700
“Six-four-seven, six-four-seven!”

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:26.719
The number is a little nod to the area code
in Toronto where movies 2-9 were filmed.

00:08:26.719 --> 00:08:30.100
It’s kind of frustrating that Jigsaw could
have been like the ultimate crime fighting

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:32.970
machine and instead became the ultimate crime
committing machine.

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:36.690
I mean he could have easily just taken these
photos to the police… same with the videotape

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:37.690
of Ivan.

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:39.820
But no, he’s gotta go and put them in literal
torture devices.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:44.100
So Rigg follows the path that Jigsaw set out
for him while Strahm also goes down the trail

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:47.900
of Jigsaw’s clues, but also stops to interrogate
Jill Tuck, at the beginning of which he looks

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.550
like he’s posing for a CZsWorld Instagram
photo.

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.240
*metal music*

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.640
She tells him more stories about John’s
past including some that took place at her

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:03.640
drug clinic, where we see younger versions
of some of the criminals from Jigsaw’s traps.

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:07.380
Gus Colyard, the guy who was taken out by
the Magnum Eyehole trap in Saw 2, gets into

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.260
a fight in the waiting room; Addison Corday,
the woman who fell victim to the Razor Box

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:15.350
trap, tries to get John to solicit prostitution
from her as he’s waiting outside; and Troy,

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:19.230
the man who met his demise in the classroom
trap in Saw 3, also seems to be a patient

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.000
or at least be connected to a patient of the
Homeward Bound Clinic.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.480
The phase of Rigg’s trial brings him to
a different classroom, where he discovers

00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:28.340
where a husband and wife are impaled with
a series of spikes.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.730
I think there’s a common misconception here,
which people love to clown on, that the woman

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:34.560
is being tested because she allowed her husband
to abuse her.

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.440
The way Jigsaw phrases it in the tape does
kind of make it seem like that is the case,

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:41.910
but I think people forget that this is the
same couple from the elementary school flashback,

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:45.540
so it was probably more about the woman keeping
quiet knowing that her husband was abusing

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:46.540
the students.

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:47.850
"He's done this before.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:48.850
She's allowing it."

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.810
I’m not saying that makes it any better,
or that she deserves to be punished or anything,

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:54.590
but I’m just trying to explain what might
have been going through Jigsaw’s head.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:57.950
I can’t really explain what was going through
Strahm’s head during the quick flashes while

00:09:57.950 --> 00:09:59.280
he’s interrogating Jill.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.500
*dramatic horn*

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:09.840
Yeah… that’s weird… should we, like…
call someone?

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:13.450
Throughout the series there have been traps
that were inspired by ancient torture devices,

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:17.150
and you couldddd say that The Knife Chair
that John puts Cecil into was one of them,

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:21.610
though it’s not exactly the same as this
Chinese torture chair from the early 1900s,

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:24.790
because Jigsaw’s version gives the victim
the ability to decide if they want to hurt

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.020
themselves in order pass.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:30.270
There’s also a trap we see in John’s workshop,
which doesn’t get used until a future movie.

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:34.020
The Glass Coffin would come back in Saw 5
and a key around Jill’s neck during the

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.740
scenes that take place in 2006, would also
return in a future film.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.400
There’s another Saw Horror History episode
coming this Wednesday, then it looks like

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.950
I’ll have to put the Saw content on hold
for a little bit while we wait for Spiral

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:48.260
to get a new release date, so if you want
to make sure you’re notified when I resume

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:53.270
with even more Things You Missed in Saw 5,
6, 7, Jigsaw and Spiral then make sure you

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:58.250
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every
week, ring that deathbell for notifications

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.300
and I’ll see you in the next one.

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:02.180
Assuming we both survive.

